          "text": "Now I have a good understanding of the current state. Let me understand the situation better:\n\nCurrent state:\n- Makefile exists and works for task 1 (both C and C++), with:\n  - CONFIG variable (debug/release)\n  - BINDIR, TESTDIR\n  - toolchain vars for MSVC (and a TODO for non-Windows)\n  - C/C++ object rules with pattern rules (`$(BINDIR)\\\\%.obj`)\n  - static geometry linking for t1\n  - exe link rules for t1 cpp and c\n  - run_case.exe tool for exit-code testing\n  - test rules for t1 (6 exit-code tests + norm output test with findstr) for both cpp and c\n  - .ok file stamps\n  - phony targets: all, t1, test, format, format-check, clean\n  - exit code variables USAGE/DATA/NO_INPUT/IO_FAIL\n\nThe .bat files for t2, t3, t4 still exist and are the current way to build/test those tasks.\n\nThe t2 uses static linking of string_utils. t3 and t4 use DLL (common_cpp.dll/common_c.dll) with COMMON_EXPORTS and PATH prepend.\n\nNow, the task is: continue integrating GNU make into the project. Currently only done for task 1 on C/C++. So the goal is to extend the Makefile to tasks 2, 3, 4.\n\nKey considerations for the plan:\n\n1. **Task 2 (t2_passport)**: static link string_utils (no geometry). Tests: norm case with Russian text output (findstr check), EOF name (66), empty name (65), EOF vertexes (66), empty vertexes (65), negative vertexes (64), fractional vertexes (65), nan vertexes (65). Russian text in tests — findstr + encoding problem (CP1251 vs OEM). This is the big complication: the .bat does DEFtoOEM conversion before findstr.\n\n2. **Task 3 (t3_bbox)**: DLL-based common (common_cpp.dll/common_c.dll) with COMMON_EXPORTS. Uses string_utils + geometry. Tests: empty case (66), not-digit case (65), too-few-args (65), 3 acceptance tests with Russian output text checks, and mass inputs with gen_cloud (10/100/10^6) — ref.ps1 as reference.\n\n3. **Task 4 (t4_filter)**: DLL-based common. Tests: radius arg errors (64), nan point (65), too few coords (65), empty coords (66), 3 acceptance output tests. gen_cloud mass inputs + ref.ps1 reference.\n\nImportant architectural points the Makefile will need:\n\n- **Static vs DLL for common**: t1 and t2 use static; t3 and t4 use DLL. This is a \"architectural axis\" per § 4.2. Need to think about how the Makefile handles this. In .bat, t3/t4 build common as DLL with COMMON_EXPORTS and the exe links against common.lib, and PATH includes build\\common so the DLL is found at runtime. Also needs the DLL in the test recipes since make test runs the exe via cmd.\n\n  Actually wait — t1 in the .bat originally used static link to geometry. t2 uses static string_utils. t3/t4 use DLL. The Makefile currently does t1 static. For t2, it would be static too. For t3/t4 — DLL.\n\n  Hmm, but there's a subtlety: The Makefile pattern rule for objects compiles with $(CXXFLAGS) which does NOT include /DCOMMON_STATIC. The t1 geometry rules explicitly add /DCOMMON_STATIC. For t3/t4 we'd need /DCOMMON_EXPORTS for the common objects and the DLL link. Also the main exe for t3/t4 must NOT define COMMON_STATIC (so COMMON_API becomes dllimport) and links with common.lib.\n\n  So there are two modes for the common objects: static (t1, t2) and dll-export (t3, t4). The Makefile needs to handle both. This is a real design decision — like the toolchain conditionals. Options:\n  - Separate object directories per mode (e.g. build/debug/common_cpp_static/ and build/debug/common_cpp_dll/), or\n  - Compile common objects twice into different names.\n  \n  In the current .bat, everything goes into the same build\\common folder, which means t2's static string_utils_cpp.obj gets overwritten by t3's dll-export string_utils_cpp.obj (that's why deepseek earlier said \"не смешивай всё в build\" and then there was the restructure). But with make, we can't overwrite — a single file target can only have one recipe. So we MUST have separate object files for the static vs dll variants. This is a genuine architectural axis.\n\n  Actually wait — in the Makefile for t1, the static geometry_cpp.obj goes to build\\debug\\common\\geometry_cpp.obj. If t2 also needs static string_utils, and t3 needs DLL string_utils + geometry, then we need:\n  - build\\debug\\common\\geometry_cpp.obj (static, for t1)\n  - build\\debug\\common\\string_utils_cpp.obj (static, for t2)\n  - build\\debug\\common_dll\\geometry_cpp.obj (dll-export, for t3/t4)\n  - build\\debug\\common_dll\\string_utils_cpp.obj (dll-export, for t3/t4)\n  \n  And similarly for C.\n\n  This is a key design point for the plan.\n\n- **Russian text in tests**: findstr has the OEM/CP1251 problem. In the .bat, they convert the output file to OEM via powershell before findstr. In Makefile test recipes, this is a multi-command sequence. Options:\n  - Keep the powershell conversion in the recipe (multi-line recipe with &&).\n  - Move string comparison logic into a helper test tool (like run_case but for text). Actually § 4.9 п.2 says \"логика длиннее одной строчки пишется тестом на C/C++\". The encoding-conversion + findstr is getting long. A cleaner approach might be a small \"check_text\" tool that takes expected substring and a file, reads both in the right codepage... but reading a CP1251 file in C++ and comparing with a CP1251 literal embedded in the source is actually simpler and avoids the OEM mess entirely! The .bat's powershell dance exists only because findstr runs in the console codepage (OEM). A C++ helper that reads the file bytes (in CP1251) and does a substring search against a CP1251 literal directly would be deterministic and cross-platform.\n\n  That's a genuine suggestion to consider. But it might be \"over-engineering\" vs just reproducing the powershell+findstr dance in make. Per § 4.9 п.2, the rule is: short one-liners OK in recipes, longer logic → C/C++ test exe. The powershell conversion + findstr is 2-3 commands. Hmm.\n\n  Actually there's a subtlety: make recipes run in cmd.exe via the recipe shell. Each recipe line is a separate cmd invocation unless joined with &&. So multi-line recipes with && work. The powershell Set-Content dance can be one (long) line. So a recipe could be:\n  \n  ```\n  $(RUN_CASE) \"...\" $(EXIT_CODE) && powershell -Command \"...\" && $(GREP) /C:\"...\" file\n  ```\n  \n  That's long but doable. But honestly a text-check helper exe would be cleaner and reusable across t2/t3/t4.\n\n- **DLL runtime PATH for test recipes**: t3/t4 exe needs common_cpp.dll on PATH at runtime. In .bat they prepend build\\common to PATH. In make recipe, each line is a fresh cmd; need to set PATH inline in the recipe or use cmd /c. Actually each recipe line is executed by cmd /c already (make uses cmd.exe as shell on Windows). Setting PATH needs to happen in the same cmd line as the program run. So the recipe line would be like:\n  \n  ```\n  set \"PATH=$(BINDIR)\\common;%PATH%\" && $(T4_CPP_EXE) ...\n  ```\n  \n  Hmm, but %PATH% in make's recipe is a make variable? No — %PATH% is cmd's environment variable. In a recipe, `%PATH%` would be expanded by cmd at runtime. But make itself also processes %...% ? No, make doesn't expand %...%. cmd does. Actually there's a catch: make will pass the recipe to cmd. Inside recipe, `%PATH%` is expanded by cmd. But wait — make recipes go through `cmd.exe /c` and %var% expansion happens. However, there might be issues with make treating `%` specially? No, % is not special in GNU make (only in some Windows make flavors). OK.\n\n  Actually there's an important subtlety: when make runs a recipe, on Windows GNU make uses cmd.exe with /c. The recipe line `set \"PATH=...;%PATH%\" && prog` would work. But actually we could also just copy the dll next to the exe, or add the dll dir... no, adding to PATH per recipe line is simplest. Or: since DLL search happens at load time, we could run via `cmd /c` with PATH. Hmm, this is a detail to decide during implementation.\n\n- **t2 ref.cpp**: t2 has ref.cpp (gitignored). Not used by make currently. The .bat t2 does NOT use ref — it uses findstr against main_out with expected literal text. OK.\n\n- **gen_cloud + ref.ps1 for mass tests (t3, t4)**: § 6.1 requires mass inputs (10/100/10^6) with generator + independent reference. The .bat t3/t4 currently don't run mass tests (the ref.ps1 files exist but the .bat doesn't call them!). Wait, let me check... In t3_build_and_test.bat, I don't see gen_cloud being built or used, and no ps1 call. Similarly t4. So the mass-input tests exist as infra (gen_cloud, ref.ps1) but aren't wired into the .bat test chains. Hmm, actually per journal notes they were written but perhaps the bat doesn't run them. Let me re-check... In t3 .bat: no gen_cloud, no ref.ps1. So mass tests are missing entirely! That's a gap.\n\n  Actually wait — § 6.1 says \"Массовый вход (задачи 3–4): генератор облака точек gen_cloud --size --seed\". These should be part of the test chain. The .bat doesn't have them. So when migrating to make, this is an opportunity to add the mass-input tests properly, as make goals (data as graph nodes per § 5 layer 15).\n\n- **.bat files removal**: § 4.9 п.1 / слой 13 — \"переходный сценарий удаляется, когда появляется сборщик\". Once make covers all 4 tasks, the .bat files should be removed. And README updated.\n\n- **Encoding of Makefile itself**: The Makefile is saved in CP1251 (comments are mojibake in my UTF-8 read). The comments in Makefile are in Russian/CP1251. This is fine per the project standard (CP1251). But note: make reads the Makefile as bytes; comments in CP1251 are fine. Keep consistent. Actually wait — let me double check: the read shows mojibake meaning Makefile is CP1251. And .editorconfig — charset unset. OK, keep CP1251. But there's a subtlety: cmd.exe /c with make — make on Windows... GNU make 4.4.1 via ezwinports. It reads Makefile fine with CP1251 comments.\n\n- **Header dependencies**: § 5 layer 15 notes that on MSVC, deps are manual (there's no -MMD; /showIncludes requires parsing). So object rules should list their headers as prerequisites manually. Currently t1's geometry rule lists both .cpp and .hpp. The generic pattern rule only lists the source, not headers. For t1, headers of main (exit_codes.hpp) aren't listed — stale obj risk. For proper behavior we'd add header prereqs. But at \"учебный масштаб\" maybe keep manual header lists per module.\n\n- **CONFIG=release**: the Makefile already handles debug/release flags. Fine.\n\n- **`all` target**: currently `all: t1`. Should become all 4 tasks. `test` should include all tests.\n\n- **run_case**: for t2/t3/t4 the exit-code tests with stdin piping can use run_case.exe similarly. For tests where the program reads args (t4 radius), the command string includes args.\n\n- **String-check tool**: The cleanest approach aligned with § 4.9 п.2 and avoiding the OEM/findstr mess is to extend the test tooling. Actually, we could enhance run_case.cpp to also do a text check (like `check_output.exe <expected-substring> <file>` reading bytes in CP1251). This would make recipes short:\n  ```\n  $(RUN_CASE) \"echo ... | $(T2_CPP_EXE) > $(BINDIR)\\t2_passport_cpp\\main_out.txt 2> nul\" 0 && $(CHECK) \"Фигура «звезда»: 6 вершин.\" $(BINDIR)\\t2_passport_cpp\\main_out.txt\n  ```\n  where CHECK is a small exe that does a raw byte substring search. This removes the powershell dance entirely and is cross-platform. This is a real improvement worth proposing.\n\n  But careful — this is a \"логика длиннее одной строчки → тест-exe\" case, so it fits the rules. And it also solves the findstr-in-OEM problem properly rather than via a hack.\n\n  Alternatively keep findstr+conversion to stay closer to existing .bat behavior. I should present this as a design decision to Ирина.\n\nLet me also check: does t3/t4 main output go to stdout with Russian text? Yes. And the findstr searches for exact substring lines. The substring search in bytes: the literal in the source is CP1251; the output file is CP1251 bytes. Byte substring match works. \n\nNow, what about stderr text checks? t3/t4 check err.txt too. Same approach — CHECK tool on err file.\n\nLet me also verify: t2 main takes name from first line (echo ...) and vertex count from second line. The .bat uses `(echo ������ & echo 6) | ...`. In make recipe, parentheses in cmd: `(echo звезда & echo 6) | prog` — needs to be within the run_case command string. run_case takes command as argv[1], which goes through make quoting. Since run_case is itself invoked by make (cmd /c \"...\"), and inside run_case.cpp it does std::system(argv[1]) — double nesting of quoting. There could be quoting hell with `&` and parens and Russian text. Actually run_case does system(command) which spawns another cmd. So the command string passed to run_case contains cmd syntax. That's fine.\n\nWait, but for the norm test the program outputs Russian text. run_case's command string would contain Russian literals too. Encoding: make passes recipe as CP1251 bytes; cmd handles them if chcp 1251. Hmm, but chcp in recipe — run_case.cpp calls system() which inherits the console codepage of the make process. This is getting complex. This is exactly the kind of thing that's a \"make on Windows with non-ASCII\" minefield. \n\nI think the plan should note this as a risk area and possibly test early with a minimal case (echo Russian | ... ) to validate before building all tests.\n\nLet me think about what the actual task scope is. Ирина says: \"продолжаю внедрять make в свой проект, пока сделан только для задачи 1 на с/с++\". So the goal is: extend the Makefile to tasks 2, 3, 4 (build + test chains), possibly gen_cloud and mass tests, remove .bat files, update README.\n\nThis is a substantial multi-step task. I should present a plan. Given plan mode, I need to research enough and then present a plan. Let me check a few more things:\n\n1. t2 main.cpp includes — to know module deps.\n2. t3/t4 main.cpp — deps.\n3. common/string_utils.cpp and geometry.cpp — to understand DLL vs static.\n4. Check if gen_cloud build is needed in Makefile.\n\nLet me read those.\n\nAlso I should double-check the current Makefile encoding. The read tool decoded as something. Let me check with a byte read of the Makefile — it says CP1251 comments. Actually the \"==========\" ASCII headers show. The comments are mojibake. So Makefile is CP1251. Keep it CP1251 for consistency.\n\nLet me read the source files quickly to know includes/deps.\n\nActually, let me not over-read. The key dependency facts I already know from .bat:\n- t2: main.cpp + string_utils (static), links string_utils_cpp.obj\n- t3: main.cpp + common.dll (string_utils + geometry) via common_cpp.lib\n- t4: main.cpp + common.dll via common_cpp.lib\n- t1: main.cpp + geometry (static)\n- gen_cloud: standalone, uses exit_codes.hpp\n- run_case: standalone test tool\n\nNow let me verify by grepping includes in the mains.",
