          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Let me now understand the state of the project fully. Let me look at the current Makefile more closely and understand what's been done and what needs doing.\n\nCurrent state from Makefile:\n- t1 done for c/cpp: obj rules, link rules, tests (exit-code via run_case + a \"norm\" string check via findstr)\n- Variables: CONFIG (debug/release), BINDIR, TESTDIR\n- Toolchain vars for MSVC, else TODO block for non-Windows\n- exit codes as variables USAGE=64, DATA=65, NO_INPUT=66, IO_FAIL=74\n- .PHONY: all t1 test format format-check clean\n- format/format-check via clang-format\n\nThe task: \"продолжаю внедрять его (make) в свой проект, пока сделан только для задачи 1 на с/с++\".\n\nSo she wants to extend make to tasks 2, 3, 4.\n\nLet me understand the differences between tasks:\n\n**Task 1 (t1_dist_matrix)**: \n- uses geometry (static link, /DCOMMON_STATIC)\n- tests: exit codes for various inputs + norm string check via findstr on a \"main_out.txt\"\n\n**Task 2 (t2_passport)**:\n- uses string_utils (static link /DCOMMON_STATIC)\n- Tests include Russian text checks via findstr (with encoding gymnastics DEFtoOEM because findstr works in OEM, files in CP1251)\n- Input uses multiple lines: (echo имя & echo 6)\n- Exit codes: 66 (eof name), 65 (empty name), 66 (eof vertexes), 65 (empty vertexes), 64 (negative), 65 (fractional), 65 (abc)\n\n**Task 3 (t3_bbox)**:\n- uses string_utils + geometry as DLL (COMMON_EXPORTS), links against common.lib, PATH needs build\\common\n- gen_cloud generates mass inputs (--size --seed)\n- tests with Russian text checks, err.txt checks\n- needs reference: t3_bbox_ref.ps1 (independent reference, on the fly)\n\n**Task 4 (t4_filter)**:\n- same DLL structure as t3\n- argc/argv radius argument parsing\n- tests with Russian text checks, err.txt checks\n- needs reference: t4_filter_ref.ps1\n\nWait — there's an interesting design decision here: t1/t2 use static libs, t3/t4 use DLLs. The .bat files reflect this: t1/t2 build with /DCOMMON_STATIC and link obj directly; t3/t4 build with /DCOMMON_EXPORTS to make common_cpp.dll + common_cpp.lib, link main against the .lib, and set PATH.\n\nBut the current Makefile only has t1 static geometry.\n\nSo the plan needs to extend make to t2 (static string_utils), t3 (DLL + gen_cloud + ref.ps1), t4 (DLL + ref.ps1).\n\nKey issues to consider:\n\n1. **Encoding problem in tests**: The .bat files use findstr + powershell Set-Content encoding conversion (DEF→OEM). In make, the recipe lines run via cmd. Russian text in findstr arguments is problematic (findstr takes args in OEM). In .bat they solve by converting file to OEM. But in make the same approach would need the powershell one-liners. Alternatively — since the tests reference Russian literals, maybe a better approach is to do the string checks in a C++ helper (like run_case but check substring). Actually per § 4.9 п.7, tests should check stdout/stderr/exit-code. The string checks in the .bat are \"grep-like\" checks. \n\n   Actually wait — this is exactly the kind of place where the course rules say: \"логика длиннее одной строчки пишется тестом на C/C++\". The findstr+Powershell-encoding dance is script-logic, and on Linux it'd be grep. A cleaner cross-platform approach might be a small checker exe (like run_case) that reads a file and checks it contains a substring, handling CP1251 as bytes. But run_case already exists for exit codes. Hmm.\n\n   But we shouldn't over-engineer. Let me think about what's the minimal honest approach consistent with the course: make recipes are one-liners (via variables). The string checks can use `findstr` like now, but with the encoding problem. On the t1 norm test, they use findstr /C:\"   0.000   1.732   1.732\" which is pure ASCII — no encoding problem. That's why t1 string test is simple.\n\n   For t2/t3/t4, the string checks involve Russian. In .bat they do the powershell encoding roundtrip. In make recipe this becomes one long line. It's ugly but works. Alternatively, refactor: use ref.exe/ref.ps1 as the reference and compare full outputs with `fc`... but the .bat explicitly moved AWAY from fc to findstr (per journal 2026-07-13: \"сравнения переделала на findstr, fc убрала в задачах 1-4\"). Hmm why? Probably because of the encoding issues with fc comparing CRLF or something. Actually fc had trouble perhaps.\n\n   Wait, actually there's a deeper issue. Let me reconsider. The task says tests for t3/t4 use gen_cloud for mass inputs and ref.ps1 as reference on the fly. The .bat files for t3/t4 do NOT actually use gen_cloud or ref.ps1! Let me check... In t3_build_and_test.bat and t4_build_and_test.bat, I don't see gen_cloud being invoked or ref.ps1 being called. The README says tests use ref for t1... Let me verify.\n\n   Looking at t3 bat: no gen_cloud invocation, no ref.ps1 call. So the mass-input tests (10/100/10^6 with gen_cloud) were part of the task spec (§ 6.1) but maybe not yet implemented in the .bat test chains. Hmm. Actually the journal for 2026-06-30/07-01 says \"gen_cloud к 3 zadche\", \"power shell референс к 3 задаче\". So gen_cloud and ref.ps1 exist but the bat doesn't call them. So mass tests are planned but not yet wired.\n\n   Actually let me re-read: § 6.1 requires for tasks 3-4: mass input via gen_cloud (10/100/10^6), reference via ref.ps1. And the README documents gen_cloud. So extending make to t3/t4 should include gen_cloud as a make target and the mass tests with reference comparison.\n\n2. **DLL vs static for t3/t4**: The Makefile needs rules to build common_cpp.dll/common_c.dll (with /DCOMMON_EXPORTS) and link main against the import lib. Also PATH handling at test time. Also the DLL must be placed where the loader can find it — the .bat sets PATH to build\\common. In make recipes, each recipe line is a separate cmd invocation, so `set PATH=...` needs to be combined with the command in the same line, or use `cmd /c \"set PATH=... && prog\"`. Actually make runs recipes via cmd.exe /c already (each line its own shell). So `set \"PATH=...;%PATH%\" && $(T4_CPP_EXE) ...` works in one recipe line. Or better: copy the dll next to the exe, or use full path. Actually simplest cross-platform-ish: since the DLL is in build\\common and exe in build\\t4..., could copy dll to each task dir, but that duplicates. Or use PATH in recipe line. That's what bat does.\n\n   Actually, wait. There's a subtlety: on Windows, the loader searches: exe dir, system dirs, current dir, PATH. The exe is in build\\t4_filter_cpp, dll in build\\common. So not found unless PATH set. The bat uses PATH approach. In make, we can prepend PATH in the test recipes.\n\n3. **gen_cloud as a target**: build gen_cloud from gen_cloud\\main.cpp. It includes \"..\\common\\exit_codes.hpp\". It uses COMMON_STATIC? No — it only uses exit_codes.hpp, no COMMON_API functions, so no define needed. Compile with plain flags.\n\n4. **Ref executables**: t1/t2 have ref.cpp/ref.c (gitignored). The .bat no longer uses ref (commented out). t3/t4 use ref.ps1 (gitignored!). Wait, t3_bbox_ref.ps1 and t4_filter_ref.ps1 are in .gitignore — they're NOT in the repo! They exist locally but are gitignored. Hmm, that's a problem for the \"independent reference\" story: § 4.9 п.3 says эталон генерятся на лету, в репо не хранятся. So ref.ps1 being gitignored is consistent with \"эталон на лету\" — but then it's not reproducible from a clean clone. Actually the course rule says: \"В репозитории — только исходники (генератор, программа, референс)\". Wait: \"В репозитории — только исходники (генератор, программа, референс) и сборочные файлы.\" So the reference SOURCE should be in repo. The ref.ps1 IS source. But it's gitignored. Hmm, contradiction. Actually maybe they decided the ref is PowerShell one-liner / separate exe and keep it local. This is a nuance to discuss.\n\n   Actually the .gitignore has t1_dist_matrix_cpp/ref.cpp etc gitignored — because in an early commit they removed refs from repo (journal 2026-07-23: \"убрала референсы из удалённого репозитория через git rm --cached ... и добавила их в .gitignore\"). So refs are intentionally not in repo. OK.\n\n   Hmm, but the current Makefile test for t1 norm doesn't use ref either — it uses findstr on expected substring. So the trend is: string checks via findstr on expected substrings, not full ref comparison.\n\n5. **run_case** — the exit-code checker. It takes a command string and expected exit code, runs via std::system, compares. This is the \"goła\" unit-test approach for exit codes.\n\nSo what is the actual current state of make integration? t1 fully wired (build + all tests: 6 exit-code tests + 1 norm string test for each of c/cpp). gen_cloud not yet a target. t2/t3/t4 not yet in make.\n\nThe task at hand: \"продолжаю внедрять его в свой проект, пока сделан только для задачи 1 на с/с++\". So extend to tasks 2, 3, 4 (and possibly gen_cloud).\n\nGiven the plan-mode instruction, I should produce a plan. But also there are ambiguities/trade-offs to surface:\n\n- **Encoding handling for Russian string checks in make recipes.** This is the biggest architectural decision. Options:\n  a. Keep findstr + powershell encoding roundtrip in make recipes (like bat does). One-liners per recipe line. Works on Windows only (fine — Windows stack). Reproduces existing behavior.\n  b. Introduce a small checker tool (like run_case but `check_text <file> <substring>` reading CP1251 bytes) to do substring checks cross-platform, avoiding the OEM mess. This is arguably \"логика длиннее одной строчки → тест-exe\" per § 4.9 п.2. And it makes recipes short one-liners. But it's a new tool.\n  \n  Hmm. Actually, a cleaner option: make the reference comparison approach — run the program and ref.ps1 on gen_cloud output and compare files with `fc`. But ref.ps1 is gitignored and not available on clean machine. And fc on Russian CP1251 text is byte-comparison — fine if both produce identical bytes. But the program output vs PowerShell output formatting may differ (line endings, trailing spaces). The current bat avoids this by findstr on specific substrings. \n\n  I think the honest recommendation is: keep the same test style as the .bat (exit-code via run_case, substring checks), but decide how to do substring checks in make recipes. Given the course's rule about \"logic longer than one line → write as C/C++ test exe\", a tiny `check_contains.exe` (or extend run_case to optionally check output file) would be the \"industrial\" approach and cross-platform. But that's adding infra. \n\n  Let me not over-decide. This is a good question for the user.\n\n- **Static (t1/t2) vs DLL (t3/t4) common module.** The Makefile currently builds static geometry for t1. For t3/t4 we need the DLL path (COMMON_EXPORTS, common.dll, import lib, PATH at runtime). Need separate obj files for static vs dll builds of the same source? The bat uses SAME obj names (build\\common\\string_utils_cpp.obj) but compiles once with COMMON_EXPORTS for t3/t4 and with COMMON_STATIC for t1/t2. That means they get overwritten — in bat sequential runs it's fine because each bat only compiles what it needs. But in make, t1/t2 need geometry/string_utils with COMMON_STATIC, t3/t4 need same sources with COMMON_EXPORTS. If we use the same obj path for both, make would conflict (one rule per target, would be overwritten — rebuild dependency issues). So we need to split obj paths: e.g. build/debug/common/geometry_cpp.obj (static) vs build/debug/common_dll/geometry_cpp.obj (dll). This is an architectural decision already foreshadowed in the journal (deepseek said name dll _c and _cpp, and separate dirs). Actually journal says dll will be common_cpp.dll and common_c.dll. And separate artifact dirs per task.\n\n  Actually wait — do t1/t2 really need static, or could all use DLL? The task § 4.7... no. The bat deliberately used static for t1/t2 and DLL for t3/t4 (that was the layer 9 exercise: static link first, then DLL). So we should preserve that distinction in make — it's part of the learning (слой 9). So make must support both static and DLL variants of the common module. That means distinct obj targets.\n\n  Options:\n  - obj paths: `$(BINDIR)\\common\\geometry_cpp.obj` for static, `$(BINDIR)\\common_dll\\geometry_cpp.obj` for dll export. DLL lib: `$(BINDIR)\\common\\common_cpp.lib` + dll. Then link t3/t4 main against common_cpp.lib.\n  - This mirrors what journal says: \"артефакты будут идти в build, который будет иметь поддиректории для каждой задачи. и dll тогда будет разделяться на _c и _cpp\".\n\n  So: common objects for static t1/t2 live in `$(BINDIR)\\common` (as now), and for DLL t3/t4 in `$(BINDIR)\\common_dll` or similar (with _c/_cpp dlls). Hmm — actually one dll can serve both t3 and t4 (c and cpp variants separate). common_cpp.dll used by t3_cpp and t4_cpp. common_c.dll by t3_c and t4_c.\n\n- **gen_cloud as make target** and mass tests (10/100/1e6) with ref.ps1 comparison. Currently gen_cloud not wired. This is part of § 6.1 for tasks 3-4. Should be added as make test data nodes (§ 5 layer 15: \"данные тестов — тоже узлы графа\").\n\n- **Which tests to port**: The bat files have many tests each (t2: 7, t3: 6+, t4: 13+). Should make port ALL of them? The current make t1 test set is 6 exit-code tests + 1 norm string test (findstr). The bat t1 had the same (norm, abc, empty, 2.3, low, high). So make t1 test set ≈ bat t1 test set. For t2/t3/t4 we should port the full bat test chains. That's a lot of .ok targets. Could use foreach/eval to generate them? That's advanced make. Or write them out. Hmm.\n\n  Actually — the course says test data should be graph nodes. Writing ~30 .ok rules by hand in make is verbose but honest. Using `$(foreach)` with pattern rules or generated rules via `define/eval` is the \"industrial\" way but complex and error-prone on Windows make. For a first pass, explicit rules are clearer to learn. Maybe recommend explicit first, then consider factorizing.\n\n- **Release config**: Makefile already handles CONFIG=release. Keep