ith a CP1251 literal compiled into the source avoids all encoding issues — both are CP1251 bytes, byte-for-byte comparison works. This is cleaner.\n\nBut wait — do we want to write our own string-check test exe, or reuse run_case? run_case only checks exit codes. We could extend to also check output. Hmm, that's a decision for the user.\n\nLet me not over-design. Let me present the options.\n\n3. **DLL vs static for t3/t4**: The current Makefile only does static. t3/t4 use DLLs. Need to add DLL build rules: compile with /DCOMMON_EXPORTS, link /DLL, produce .dll + import .lib + .exp, then link main.exe against import lib, and ensure runtime PATH includes build\\common so the DLL is found. In Makefile recipes for tests, need to set PATH. Actually in .bat they do `set \"PATH=%~dp0/build/common;%PATH%\"`. In make, each recipe is a new cmd, so we need `set PATH=...;%PATH% && ...` within each test recipe that runs the exe.\n\nHmm, but wait — should tasks 3-4 keep using DLL? Per § 5 layer 9, the DLL step was done. The course says \"cl /LD utils.cpp → .dll + import-.lib, линковка потребителя через import-lib — обязательный пункт фундамента\". So yes, keep DLL for t3/t4 to preserve the layer in make.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: the obj names conflict. In .bat for t3/t4, string_utils_cpp.obj is compiled with /DCOMMON_EXPORTS. In t2 .bat, string_utils_cpp.obj is compiled with /DCOMMON_STATIC. Same obj name, different flags — in a single build tree, that's a conflict! The Makefile currently has build/debug/common/string_utils_cpp.obj... but no rule for string_utils at all yet (only geometry for t1). \n\nSo when extending to t2 (needs static string_utils) and t3/t4 (need exported string_utils+geometry), the obj naming must disambiguate: e.g., geometry_cpp.obj (static, for t1) vs geometry_cpp_dll.obj or a separate subdir for DLL-exported objects. This is an architectural axis to flag.\n\nActually the README mentions the planned structure: build/debug/common has geometry_cpp.obj, string_utils_c.pdb etc. And dll will be split into _c and _cpp. From the journal 2026-07-28: \"dll тогда будет разделяться на _c и _cpp\".\n\nSo plan should decide: static objs (t1: geometry, t2: string_utils) vs dll objs (t3/t4: string_utils+geometry exported). Distinct object paths.\n\nOptions:\n- static: build/debug/common/geometry_cpp.obj, string_utils_cpp.obj\n- dll: build/debug/common_dll/geometry_cpp.obj, string_utils_cpp.obj, then common_cpp.dll, common_cpp.lib in build/debug/common_dll/ (or build/debug/common/).\n\nGiven PATH handling, maybe put dll in build/debug/common_dll and set PATH accordingly. Or simpler: name objs with suffix.\n\n4. **test target semantics**: Currently `test: format-check $(T1_CPP_TESTS) $(T1_C_TESTS)`. Extend with t2/t3/t4 test lists. Also there's a question of whether the run_case mechanism (std::system) handles the DLL PATH. run_case runs cmd.exe internally (std::system), inheriting the environment of run_case, which inherits make's environment. Since make recipes can set PATH inline for the run_case invocation, the child cmd gets it too.\n\nActually, does run_case spawn via cmd? std::system uses cmd.exe /c on Windows. It inherits the process env. If we set PATH in the recipe before invoking run_case, the env propagates. Good.\n\n5. **The format target wildcard**: `$(wildcard **/*.cpp ...)`. Note: `**` in GNU make wildcard — does it recurse? No! GNU make's `wildcard` function does not support `**` recursion; `**` is treated as `*` (two stars collapse). So `**/*.cpp` matches files one level deep, not recursive. Let me check: actually `**` in GNU make is just `*` repeated — glob `**` matches like `*`, i.e., any string. So `**/*.cpp` == `*/*.cpp` — matches cpp files in one-level subdirs. That covers t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.cpp, common/*.cpp, gen_cloud/main.cpp, tests/run_case.cpp. All source dirs are exactly one level deep, so it works by accident. Fine, but worth noting. Not a bug to fix now.\n\n6. **gen_cloud target**: For t3/t4 mass tests, need to build gen_cloud. gen_cloud/main.cpp uses only exit_codes.hpp (no COMMON_API), so plain compile, no link with common. \n\nBut do we need gen_cloud in this iteration? The .bat files for t3/t4 don't use it. Hmm. The task spec says mass input tests are part of the test chain (§ 6.1: \"Массовый вход (задачи 3–4): генератор облака точек gen_cloud --size --seed\"). So ideally the Makefile test chain for t3/t4 should include a mass test: generate cloud with gen_cloud, run main.exe, compare against ref.ps1 output.\n\nThe ref.ps1 approach: run program < cloud.txt > out.txt; run powershell ref.ps1 < cloud.txt > ref.txt; fc out.txt ref.txt. That's a few commands. In make, that's a test recipe with multiple steps. per § 4.9 п.2, \"Связка в одну строчку — допустима; логика длиннее — пишется тестом\". The ref.ps1 pipeline is arguably a single-line-ish chain. Hmm.\n\nBut note the encoding: ref.ps1 output via PowerShell with Russian literals — PowerShell writes to stdout in OEM? When redirected, PowerShell uses the console codepage... This could produce encoding mismatch with main.exe's CP1251 output. The .bat avoided this by not using ref.ps1 in chains. Risky territory.\n\nAlternative: write the reference as a small C++ exe (like t1 had ref.cpp). But t1's ref was removed/gitignored. Actually .gitignore has t1_dist_matrix_cpp/ref.cpp and ref.c ignored, t2 refs ignored, and t3/t4 ref.ps1 ignored! Interesting — the refs are NOT committed (gitignored). Wait: .gitignore lines 9-14 ignore t1/t2 ref.cpp/ref.c and t3/t4 ref.ps1. So the refs exist locally but aren't in the repo. Per § 4.9 п.3, \"эталон считает независимая референс-реализация... Замороженный снапшот выхода — не эталон\". The ref is supposed to be in the repo as source (генератор, программа, референс). But they've gitignored them. Hmm, that's a repo-infra inconsistency, but maybe intentional from journal 2026-07-23 \"убрала референсы из удалённого репозитория\". Actually the journal says they removed references from the repo. But § 4.9 п.3 says \"В репозитории — только исходники (генератор, программа, референс)\". So keeping ref sources gitignored contradicts § 4.9 п.3. Hmm. This might be a point to raise.\n\nWait, but gitignored means they're not committed — that means on a clean clone, `make test` would fail because ref files are missing. This violates § 4.9 п.1 \"на чистом клоне до зелёных тестов — одна команда\". So there's a real issue: refs are gitignored, but tests depend on them.\n\nHmm, but the current .bat chains don't use refs — they use findstr string checks with expected literals embedded in the .bat. And the Makefile t1 norm test uses findstr on \"   0.000   1.732   1.732\" literal. So the current test style is: exit-code checks + substring checks, not ref-comparison. The refs are mostly legacy (t1/t2 refs were abandoned, commented out in .bat).\n\nSo the pragmatic plan: continue the established test style in make — exit codes via run_case + substring checks. For substring checks with Russian, we need an encoding-safe mechanism (findstr won't work directly in make recipes for Russian without the OEM dance; and the Makefile itself is CP1251 which might get mangled by cmd).\n\nHold on. Actually there's a subtlety: when make invokes cmd.exe with a recipe line, the line is passed as the command string. cmd then passes to findstr the args. findstr on Windows reads its pattern argument in the OEM codepage. So a Russian literal in the Makefile (CP1251) → cmd interprets the command line... how does the Makefile line reach cmd.exe? GNU make on Windows spawns cmd.exe /c with the recipe string. The recipe string bytes (CP1251) are converted... this is exactly the same problem as in .bat files, hence the powershell DEFtoOEM dance there. In make it's the same dance, or avoid Russian in make by doing string checks in a C++ exe.\n\nGiven the course's explicit rule § 4.9 п.2 (minimize script logic; longer logic → C/C++ test exe) and the encoding pain, I think the cleanest approach that the user might appreciate:\n\n**Extend the test harness**: write a small checker exe (like `check_out.exe`) that takes an expected substring (as an argument... but args have encoding issues too when passed via cmd). Hmm. argv encoding: the C runtime interprets argv in the ANSI codepage (CP1251) by default, so if the pattern is passed via cmd in a recipe, cmd passes the string as... the command line is in the console codepage (OEM) when the parent writes it? Actually when cmd /c is spawned by make, the command line is Unicode internally; make passes the recipe as a Unicode string? GNU make on Windows converts. This is getting deep.\n\nSimpler: the checker exe could read the expected substring from a file, or better — compare the output file with an expected-output file byte-by-byte (fc), where expected-output is generated by... no, we're back to refs.\n\nOK here's the thing — I should not over-design. Let me present options to the user and ask. The user is a student; the value is in her learning. I should ask clarifying questions about scope and approach, present the architectural axis, and let her decide.\n\nLet me structure the plan:\n\n**Scope decision** (ask): extend Makefile to t2, t3, t4 for both C and C++? Include mass-input tests via gen_cloud? Replace .bat files when make covers them?\n\n**Architecture axes to name before the plan:**\n1. Static vs DLL for common (t1/t2 static, t3/t4 DLL) → object/dir naming must disambiguate so both variants can coexist in build tree.\n2. Test assertion style: exit-code-only (run_case) + substring checks. How to do Russian substring checks in make (encoding) — options: (a) replicate powershell OEM dance in recipes, (b) small C++ check exe that compares CP1251 bytes (avoids console encoding), (c) ref.ps1 comparison.\n3. DLL runtime PATH in test recipes for t3/t4.\n4. gen_cloud as a target + mass tests (10/100/10^6).\n\nLet me also check: does the Makefile currently have `run_case` building correctly? Yes, rule