p\". Hmm.\n\nActually the .bat files compiled the same object names in build\\common regardless of static/dll — but that's a bug-ish (overwrites). In make we must have distinct object names per flavor. Let me propose:\n- Static objs: `$(BINDIR)\\common\\geometry_cpp.obj`, `$(BINDIR)\\common\\string_utils_cpp.obj` (for t1/t2)\n- DLL objs: `$(BINDIR)\\common\\geometry_cpp_dll.obj`, `$(BINDIR)\\common\\string_utils_cpp_dll.obj` (for t3/t4), then `common_cpp.dll` and import lib `common_cpp.lib` in `$(BINDIR)\\common\\`.\n\nNaming: user uses `common_cpp.dll` / `common_c.dll` in .bat (e.g., build\\common\\common_cpp.dll). And import libs `common_cpp.lib`. The Makefile should produce those.\n\nFor DLL runtime: main.exe needs the DLL on PATH. In .bat they prepend build\\common to PATH. In make recipes, each recipe line runs in its own cmd; we need `set \"PATH=...;%PATH%\"` before running. Actually since tests run via run_case which calls std::system(command), the PATH must be set in the environment inherited by run_case. We can do in recipe: `set \"PATH=$(BINDIR)\\common;%PATH%\" && $(RUN_CASE) \"...\" 0 && ...`. In cmd, `set VAR=val && cmd2` — the set affects the current cmd process and children. Yes works.\n\nAlternatively simpler: copy the DLL next to each main.exe. But user's .bat used PATH approach. Keep PATH approach — set PATH in recipes that invoke t3/t4 exes. Note: run_case inherits environment of the make recipe cmd, which has PATH set. Good.\n\nNow, the exit-code variable names. Existing: USAGE=64, DATA=65, NO_INPUT=66, IO_FAIL=74. Good — reuse.\n\nNow the pattern rules. Current:\n```\n$(BINDIR)\\\\%.obj: %.cpp | $(CPP_DIRS)\n\t$(CXX) /c /Fo:$@ $(CXXFLAGS) $<\n```\nWait — `$(BINDIR)` contains backslashes (build\\debug). `$(BINDIR)\\\\%.obj` → `build\\debug\\\\%.obj`. In make, `\\\\` is escaped backslash → literal `\\`, then `%` pattern. So `build\\debug\\%.obj` pattern matches targets like `build\\debug\\common\\geometry_cpp.obj` with stem `common\\geometry_cpp`. Then `%.cpp` → `common\\geometry_cpp.cpp` — doesn't exist (source is common\\geometry.cpp). So pattern rule would only match when the obj name == source name + .obj, i.e. task dirs main.cpp → build\\debug\\t1_dist_matrix_cpp\\main.obj matches stem `t1_dist_matrix_cpp\\main`, prereq `t1_dist_matrix_cpp\\main.cpp`. Good. For common, object names differ (geometry_cpp.obj from geometry.cpp) so explicit rules handle them. t2 main: build\\debug\\t2_passport_cpp\\main.obj ← t2_passport_cpp\\main.cpp. Works via pattern. Good.\n\nNow the dirs prerequisite. Currently `$(CPP_DIRS) $(C_DIRS) $(BINDIR)\\common $(BINDIR)\\tools $(TESTDIR)` all grouped with `$(MKDIR) $@`. We'll add t2/t3/t4 dirs, and maybe `$(BINDIR)\\test` already. Actually currently only t1 dirs. We add: t2_passport_cpp, t2_passport_c, t3_bbox_cpp, t3_bbox_c, t4_filter_cpp, t4_filter_c. And common (exists). The rule `$(CPP_DIRS) $(C_DIRS) ...:` — we add new dirs to these variable lists or to the target list. Since all these dirs use same recipe `$(MKDIR) $@`, add them to the list. But careful: the pattern rule's order-only prereq uses `$(CPP_DIRS)` — which for t1 is `$(BINDIR)\\t1_dist_matrix_cpp`. For a pattern rule `$(BINDIR)\\\\%.obj: %.cpp | $(CPP_DIRS)`, the order-only prereqs apply to ALL targets matched by the pattern (t2, t3, t4 mains too!). So `$(CPP_DIRS)` must include all cpp task dirs so that mkdir happens before compiling any matched main.obj. Actually order-only prereq just ensures it exists (created if needed). If we add all dirs to CPP_DIRS, then when building t2 main.obj, make will mkdir t2 dir first. Good. So CPP_DIRS should be the list of all cpp task dirs, C_DIRS all c dir task dirs.\n\nHmm wait, but there's an ordering subtlety the user discovered: order-only prereqs get created during update, before recipe runs. If dir doesn't exist, make runs mkdir target. That works regardless of order. Good.\n\nAlso the common DLL dir — `$(BINDIR)\\common` already in the mkdir target list. But for DLL we need it too. Fine.\n\nNow tests. The .ok stamp approach. Each task needs test targets. Number of tests: t2 has ~7 exit-code tests + norm string test per language. t3 has ~3 error cases + 3 norm string tests per language + mass tests. t4 has ~5 radius-error cases + 3 point-error cases + 3 norm + mass. That's a LOT of .ok rules. Total maybe ~40 test rules. This is getting verbose. Per § 4.9 п.2 \"Скриптовые полотна минимизированы... логика длиннее — тестом на C/C++\". And the user's journal notes the \"повторила дважды — выноси\" principle. In make, we can use `$(foreach)`/`$(eval)` to generate test rules from lists — advanced make. Or use static pattern rules. Hmm.\n\nActually, a cleaner approach: each test is a rule `$(TESTDIR)\\tX_lang_<name>.ok: $(EXE) $(RUN_CASE) $(CHECK_CONTAINS) | $(TESTDIR)` with recipe. We could generate them with foreach+eval from a per-task list of (name, command, expected-code, expected-substring). That's quite advanced for a student at layer 15. But it reduces duplication massively. However, the course approach values understanding over cleverness. Given this is her make learning, maybe better to write them explicitly but factor via variables and a `define` template? \n\nHmm. Let me think about what's pedagogically right and matches the course. The journal shows she's already dealing with make complexity. A `$(eval)` template might be too big a jump. But 40 hand-written rules is unmanageable and error-prone. A middle ground: a `define`-block recipe template invoked per test with `$(eval)`. Actually that IS $(eval). \n\nAlternatively: batch tests by shared command patterns. E.g., for each task+lang define a small set of (case, stdin-command, expected-code) triples; use a function to generate rules. I think introducing `$(foreach)` + `$(eval)` is the right industrial tool here and a great learning opportunity — but I should flag it as a \"новый приём make\" in the plan and let her decide. Let me present it as: I'll propose using eval-generated rules, but note it's a new make concept; alternative is verbose explicit rules.\n\nActually, let me reconsider scope. Maybe the plan should stage: first port t2 fully, then t3 (DLL + mass), then t4. The user said \"продолжаю внедрять make... пока сделан только для задачи 1\". So the goal is to cover tasks 2-4 in make. Let me propose the full plan but structured in commits/stages so each is testable.\n\nAlso important: format-check and clang-format run on all source. The `format` target uses `$(wildcard **/*.cpp ...)`. Note: `**` in GNU make wildcard — does GNU make support `**`? GNU make's wildcard does NOT support `**` globstar! It treats `**` as `*` (just a single star, matching within one directory level). Wait, actually `**/*.cpp` — `*` matches any sequence not including `/`; `**` = `*` `*` → two consecutive globs each matching one segment? Let me think: `**/*.cpp` with `*` matching non-slash chars: `**` would match like `*` (any name without slash), then `/`, then `*.cpp`. So `**/*.cpp` matches one directory level only (e.g. `t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.cpp`). It does NOT recurse deeper. Since all sources are at one level, it works currently. But it's fragile. The dry-run output showed it did list all source files including subdirs — wait it listed `common/geometry.cpp`, `gen_cloud/main.cpp`, `t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.cpp` etc. — all one level. Good, no deeper nesting. OK it works for now. I could mention this as a note but not critical.\n\nWait, actually GNU make wildcard: `*` matches any sequence of characters. In globbing, `*` does not cross `/`. So `**/*.cpp`: `**` is interpreted as `*` matching zero+ chars excluding `/`, so it matches e.g. `common`, then `/*.cpp`. It requires at least one directory level. Since all sources live in subdirs, works. If there were a source in root (hello/hello.cpp is in subdir). OK fine.\n\nNow, about the checker tool design. Let me design `check_contains.exe`:\n- usage: `check_contains <haystack_file> <needle_file>`\n- reads