subtle issue: gen_cloud produces points with `std::print(\"{} {} {}\\n\", ...)` — full double precision. The main programs read them via from_chars. Should round-trip. Fine.\n\nAlso: should mass tests check stderr is empty? Exit code 0 is checked via run_case or the recipe. For mass test we could run `$(T3_CPP_EXE) < cloud.txt > out.txt 2> err.txt` then check err empty? Keep simple: exit code 0 + fc out. Maybe check err.txt empty with the checker (expected empty file). Optional. I'll include exit-code + fc as core.\n\nNow let me also reconsider: the t3/t4 tests in .bat use multi-line stdin via `(echo ... & echo ...)`. In make recipes, cmd accepts that. run_case passes command string to system() which runs cmd /c. So `(echo 0 0 0 & echo 1 0 0 & echo 0 1 0) | main.exe` works. Good.\n\nEncoding: when make runs `echo` with Russian inside run_case command string — the Russian is in the Makefile (CP1251). cmd /c parses; console codepage matters. This was the known fragility. For t2 norm test (echo имя & echo 6), Russian input. Hmm — but with the checker reading output file as bytes, the OUTPUT comparison is robust. The INPUT via echo of Russian — depends on chcp at make runtime. The .bat set chcp 1251 at top. In make, recipes each spawn new cmd; we'd need `chcp 1251 > nul &&` in recipes that pipe Russian input? Actually the program reads stdin bytes; if echo outputs in OEM (default console cp) vs CP1251... The program expects CP1251. The output file bytes would then be OEM? The program just echoes the name. If console is 866 (OEM), echo produces 866 bytes for the Russian name, program stores/echoes them as-is, output file contains 866 bytes, expected file is CP1251 → mismatch!\n\nSo for Russian *input* tests we have a real encoding concern regardless of checker. Options: set `chcp 1251` in the recipe before echo (e.g., recipe starts with `chcp 1251 > nul && (echo ...) | ...`). chcp changes console input/output codepage for that cmd. That should make echo emit CP1251. Actually does echo emit based on console CP? The cmd internal echo writes bytes; chcp affects how cmd interprets. In a redirected pipe, cmd converts console CP to OEM? Ugh. This is exactly the fragility.\n\nAlternative robust approach for Russian-input tests: generate the input from a file too! E.g., commit small input data files (CP1251) and use `type input.txt | main.exe` or `< input.txt`. Then no echo-of-Russian in recipes. `main.exe < input.txt` (input redirection). This avoids echo entirely — robust bytes. The .bat used echo because it was convenient, but for make we can use data files. This aligns with § 4.9: входы — данные, не скриптовый ввод.\n\nSo plan: for tests needing Russian or multi-line stdin, use committed/generated input files (in `tests/` or per-task `tests/`), redirect `<`. Actually even ASCII multi-line could be data files. For single-line ASCII (echo 2.3) echo is fine.\n\nHmm, but this adds more committed files. Trade-off. I think it's the right call for robustness, and I'll note it. Actually — wait. Let me reconsider: does the console CP matter for `echo abc` (ASCII)? ASCII is same in all code pages, so echo fine for ASCII inputs. Only Russian inputs need files. t2 norm test inputs Russian name → use input file. t3 norm tests input numeric points → echo fine (ASCII). t4 same. Good, so only t2's norm test (and maybe t2 ref?) uses a Russian input file. Actually t2's empty/EOF cases use `type nul` and `echo;` — ASCII-ish. t2 norm: `(echo звезда & echo 6)` → needs file. And t2 ref.cpp (if used) — not needed.\n\nOK. Let me now also double check: does t2 use string_utils only (no geometry)? t2 main includes string_utils.hpp and exit_codes.hpp. Yes. t2 c uses string_utils.h. So t2 objs: main + string_utils (static). Good.\n\nt3/t4 use geometry + string_utils both (parse_point in geometry, trim/is_empty in string_utils). DLL.\n\nNow let me define the object naming in make to avoid static/dll conflicts:\n- Static (t1 geometry, t2 string_utils): `$(BINDIR)\\common\\geometry_cpp.obj`, `$(BINDIR)\\common\\string_utils_cpp.obj` — but wait, these get compiled with /DCOMMON_STATIC. Currently t1 make uses geometry_cpp.obj static. Good.\n- DLL (t3/t4): need /DCOMMON_EXPORTS objects: `$(BINDIR)\\common\\geometry_cpp_dll.obj`, `$(BINDIR)\\common\\string_utils_cpp_dll.obj`. Then:\n  - `$(BINDIR)\\common\\common_cpp.dll: geometry_cpp_dll.obj string_utils_cpp_dll.obj` → `$(LINK) /DLL /OUT:$@ $^` producing also common_cpp.lib, common_cpp.exp.\n  - main links: `$(LINK) ... main.obj common_cpp.lib`.\n  - C side: `common_c.dll` from geometry_c_dll.obj string_utils_c_dll.obj; main.c links common_c.lib.\n- PATH in t3/t4 test recipes: `set \"PATH=$(BINDIR)\\common;%PATH%\" && ...`.\n\nHmm wait, but t3/t4 C and C++ share the same DLLs? No — separate common_c.dll and common_cpp.dll (C++ std::string vs C char*). Right, separate.\n\nNow: the DLL import/export. In .bat: string_utils and geometry compiled with /DCOMMON_EXPORTS, linked /DLL into common_cpp.dll; main compiled WITHOUT COMMON_STATIC (so COMMON_API = dllimport), links with common_cpp.lib. Consistent.\n\nThe LINK_FLAGS: /DEBUG for debug. For DLL also /DLL. Note: when linking /DLL, also need /EXPORT? No, __declspec(dllexport) handles exports. Good.\n\nAlso: `run_case` uses std::system → exit code. Note Windows system() returns -1 if error; but for our purposes ok. Actually it returns the exit code *256? On Windows, system() returns the exit code directly (0-255?) Actually the C runtime returns the exit code of the process as-is? Hmm — std::system on Windows returns the exit code returned by cmd /c (which propagates the program's exit code). The t1 tests already work, so fine.\n\nNow — plan structure. The user wants to \"continue integrating make into the project, currently only for task 1\". So deliverable: Makefile covering t1-t4 (C and C++), with test chains, gen_cloud, refs, checker tool, format targets working. Plus possibly removal of .bat scripts once make covers everything (§ 4.9 п.1: \"переходный сценарий удаляется, когда появляется сборщик\"). But that's a decision — .bat removal. The journal said bat is transitional, removed when make appears. But careful: removing all .bat at once is a big change; maybe do it as the last step, or keep until make is fully verified. I'll propose: after make fully covers t2-t4 and tests pass, delete the four .bat files and update README (per § 4.9 п.1 \"одна точка входа\"). But ask? The user might want to keep them. Actually § 4.9 п.1 explicitly: \"переходный сценарий удаляется\". And § 5 слой 13: \"Живёт до сборщика, удаляется при появлении make\". So yes, remove bats. I'll include as final step. But note README still references them.\n\nAlso `.editorconfig` charset not set (per journal). Fine.\n\nAlso: `run_case` — maybe we should also add stderr checks? § 4.9 п.7: tests verify all three channels: stdout, stderr, exit code. For error cases in .bat, they checked stderr content via findstr and exit code, stdout redirected to nul. In make we can check stderr via checker (expected substring in err file). E.g., error cases: `2> err.txt`, check exit code via run_case (but run_case command string needs the redirect inside) — actually run_case runs the command and returns based on exit code; the recipe can't separately inspect err unless the command writes err to a file. Pattern: `$(RUN_CASE) \"echo abc | $(T2_CPP_EXE) > nul 2> $(BINDIR)\\test\\t2_cpp_abc.err\" $(DATA) && $(CHECK) $(BINDIR)\\test\\t2_cpp_abc.err $(EXPECT)\\t2_cpp_abc.exp && $(TOUCH) $@`. But stdout>nul loses stdout check for error cases (fine, error cases are about stderr+code). And exit code from run_case checks code. Good — three channels covered across the suite.\n\nHmm, wait: but `> $(NULLDEV)` for stdout in error tests — fine.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: run_case's command is passed as one argv to run_case, which passes to system(). The `2> file` redirect happens in cmd. Fine.\n\nNow t2 error messages: need expected substrings. E.g., from