r byte-exact fc. \n\nWait — std::reduce with std::execution::seq is deterministic sequential? Actually `std::reduce(first, last)` uses default execution policy (sequenced) and is unspecified order even then? Per cppreference: std::reduce, unless execution policy seq is specified, is non-deterministic in order. With std::execution::seq it's deterministic? Actually even with seq policy, order \"may be nondeterministic\"? Let me recall: reduce without policy — \"the behavior is non-deterministic if not specified\". With seq policy, it's sequenced but still... cppreference says for std::reduce: \"Given the binary operation is associative and commutative... otherwise non-deterministic\". Sum of doubles is NOT associative (floating point). With default (sequenced) policy, implementations typically do left fold? Not guaranteed. MSVC's std::reduce default — I believe it does a straightforward accumulate-like loop, but standard doesn't guarantee. Risk of mismatch between main (std::reduce) and ref (sequential loop). \n\nIf they mismatch at the last bit, the 3-decimal rounding could differ (rare, but possible). To be safe, the ref should mirror main's computation method, OR better: the mass test comparison should not rely on byte-exactness of centroid/avg computed by different summation orders. Options:\n(a) Use a tolerance-based comparison instead of fc for mass tests — but course says fc честен при 3 знаках. It's honest only if both compute identically.\n(b) Make ref compute the same way (e.g., ref also uses std::reduce with same policy → likely identical on same compiler/runtime). Since both C++ exes on same MSVC, std::reduce default probably compiles to same loop → identical. Plausible but not guaranteed.\n(c) Compare with the checker but using per-line matching with tolerance — too complex.\n(d) For mass test, use ref.ps1? No, encoding.\n\nActually simplest robust: implement ref to reuse the SAME summation approach (std::reduce) — then same MSVC runtime → same order → identical bytes. Since ref is also C++, fine. For t4 filter, it's per-point independent filtering, no summation → byte-exact safe. For t3 centroid+avgdist involves sums. I'll note this risk in the plan and mitigate: ref mirrors main's numeric method (or we accept and verify empirically; if mismatch, adjust). This is exactly a \"реальное столкновение с плавающей точкой\" learning point. I'll flag it.\n\nAlso for t1: no ref, substring check — fine.\n\nHmm, also there's the C main for t3 — C computes sum sequentially (hand-written loop), C++ main uses std::reduce. Comparing C main output vs C++ ref output: sequential loop vs reduce — same risk. So the reference should ideally be per-language? That doubles refs. Alternatively ref for C uses sequential loop matching C main. Ugh.\n\nActually, simpler: byte-exact fc for centroid/avg across independent implementations is fragile in principle. But the numbers are rounded to 3 decimals; small sum-order differences rarely change 3rd decimal. The course explicitly endorses 3-decimal fc. Empirically it'll likely pass for 10/100/1e6 random points. If a flaky mismatch appears, fallback: tolerance-based checker. I'll note this as a risk with fallback. Good enough for a plan.\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider. Is there value in the ref being \"independent\"? Yes (§ 4.9 п.3). But for floating sums, independence hurts byte-exactness. The course acknowledges this (\"эталон и результат сравниваются с фиксированным форматом вывода (3 знака — тогда fc честен)\"). The intent: formatted compare. OK. I'll keep ref independent (C++ ref, own loop using accumulate) and accept 3-decimal fc; flag fallback.\n\nAlright. Also: t4 filter test \"point exactly on radius\" corner (§ 6.1: точка ровно на радиусе R) — add a case: point at distance == R → excluded. In .bat? t4 test3 uses echo 3 4 0 | main.exe 5 → dist 5, not <5 → empty. That IS the boundary case (distance exactly R). Good, already covered.\n\nAlso § 6.1: t1 acceptance \"формула длины хорд