t `tests/data` — actually simpler to name them `.in` and `.exp`? `.exp` is gitignored for dll exports. Rename: `.in` and `.expect`? Hmm `.in`/`.expect` not gitignored. But data files could be .txt... Let me just use extensions that aren't gitignored: input `*.in`, expect `*.expect`? That's unusual but fine. OR add gitignore negations. I'll propose negations (`!tests/data/` style) since .txt is natural. Actually gitignore negation for files inside ignored *.txt: you must negate the dir too: `!tests/`, `!tests/data/`, `!tests/data/*.txt`. Doable. I'll note it.\n\nWait, actually there may be a simpler approach for expected substrings: since they're short, maybe the checker accepts the needle as a file, and the \"expected files\" could be generated from a committed C++ source? No — overkill.\n\nOK also consider: do we even need committed input files? The t2 norm input is `(echo звезда & echo 6)`. Could run as `echo звезда` — Russian echo fragility. File `<` is robust. Yes commit input files.\n\nAlternatively, since echo's codepage depends on chcp at make-time, we could put `chcp 1251 > nul &&` at start of that recipe. But honestly file redirection is cleaner and mirrors § 4.9 \"вход от генератора или литерал\" and the mass-input pattern. Go with files.\n\nLet me now also think: how many expect files? For each string-checked test, one. t2: norm cpp + norm c (2). t3: not_digit stderr (2: cpp/c share same message? The message text is same for c and cpp? In .bat they're same strings. Yes can share one expect file per message, but per-language files simpler and allow divergence). t4: radius errors messages (5 kinds) etc. ~15-20 small files. Fine.\n\nAlright, I think I have a thorough plan. Let me also decide commit sequence (she likes regular commits). Plan will propose ~5 commits.\n\nLet me write the plan now. I'll keep it structured but not overly verbose. Include:\n- Текущее состояние (кратко)\n- Цель\n- Архитектурные оси\n- Шаги (инфраструктура, t2, t3, t4, унификация/за