rent command. The recipes use cmd-specific syntax (`type nul`, `findstr`, `2> nul`), and they pass, so SHELL must be cmd.exe. Actually the default SHELL on Windows for GNU make when sh.exe is not found is cmd.exe. Since tests pass with `type nul`, it's cmd.exe. Good.\n\nSo the recipe is correct. However, I should note the important nuance for the user's broader plan: the reason t1 can use findstr with ASCII pattern is encoding-safe, but t2-t4 cannot (Russian literals), hence the check_contains tool. Also, this norm test uses findstr which requires the output to have the EXACT spacing — good that format is `%8.3f`.\n\nLet me also flag: the continuation `&& \\` joining — in make -n output it appeared split, which could worry the user. I should explain that make joins it into one logical command passed to cmd, and that's why `&&` works across the \"visual\" line break. Actually let me verify this is truly joined, because if make passed two cmd invocations the second line's `&&` would be lost and the test would falsely pass (findstr failing wouldn't stop touch). Hmm, wait — if they were separate cmd invocations:\n- cmd#1: `run_case \"...\" 0 && \\` → cmd would try to run `run_case \"...\" 0 && \\`. In cmd, `&& \\` at end... cmd sees `\\` as a regular char at the end of the command line. Actually cmd would run `run_case \"...\" 0 && \\` → the `\\` after `&&` would be a command `\\` which fails (\"\\ is not recognized\"), so cmd#1 exit code = 1 (from `\\`). Then `&&` in the recipe? No — make doesn't chain cmd invocations with &&. make runs cmd#1, checks its exit code; if 0, runs cmd#2. Since cmd#1 would return 1 (due to `\\`), the test would FAIL. But the .ok file exists → so it's NOT two cmd invocations; make must join them into one logical line. \n\nSo confirmed: make joins the backslash-continued recipe into a single logical line passed to cmd. The `\\` is consumed by make as line continuation. \n\nActually, hold on — I should double check GNU make's behavior of joining recipe continuation lines. Yes, GNU make manual §5.1.1 \"Splitting Recipe Lines\": \"One difference between recipes and ordinary text is that a blank line in a recipe... Another difference is that the backslash-newline continuation in a recipe... the backslash and newline are replaced with a single space, and leading whitespace of the next line is collapsed to a single space.\" Hmm, I recall the manual says: \"When a recipe line is split with a backslash-newline, the leading whitespace on the next line is NOT stripped\" — actually there's a specific note. Let me not overstate. The empirical evidence (tests pass, .ok exists) is decisive: it works.\n\nSo my answer: Yes, correct. Explain why, mention the encoding-safety of ASCII, mention the norm test only checks first row (partial), mention stderr swallowed (per §4.9 п.7 could be improved, but consistent with .bat; the C++ checker plan will address t2-t4). Also note the continuation `&& \\` is one logical line to cmd.\n\nI should also address the C++ analog — same structure, same reasoning.\n\nOne more thing to verify: the `&&` after run_case requires run_case exit 0. run_case returns 0 only if exit codes match. Good.\n\nAlso the `$(TOUCH)` = `type nul >` — creates empty .ok. If findstr fails (returns 1), the `&&` chain stops, no .ok → make reports failure. Correct: the test fails. And if run_case fails, chain stops too. Correct.\n\nEdge: does findstr return 0 when found? Yes, findstr returns 0 on match, 1 on no match, 2 on error. Good.\n\nSo overall verdict: correct, matches the .bat approach (which also used findstr on `   0.000   1.732   1.732`), tests are green. \n\nLet me give a concise answer. Since we're in plan mode and the user asked a verification question, I should answer directly and note any caveats, and mention where the plan's check_contains would replace this for t2-t4. Keep it concise.\n\nI don't need more tool calls. Answer in Russian.",
