          "text": "The user asks me to explain the concept of \"двухканальность\" (two-channel) from § 4.9 п.6 of their AGENTS.md. Let me explain it clearly, using their own project context as examples.\n\nThe quote:\n\"**Двухканальность:** exit-код — категория для скрипта-обёртки (машинный канал), сообщение в stderr — детализация для человека (системный текст ошибки + контекст операции). Антипаттерн — `return errno;`.\"\n\nLet me explain this concept well. I should explain what the two channels are, why they exist, and what the anti-pattern means.\n\nThe two channels:\n1. **Exit code** — a single integer returned by the process. This is the \"machine channel\" — consumed by a script/wrapper (cmd, make, another program). It has very limited bandwidth (0-255 on Windows / typically one byte). It can only carry a category, not details. It's the only information a script can reliably capture.\n\n2. **stderr** — text messages intended for a human. It carries the full detail: what operation failed, system error text (strerror/GetLastError), context (what values, what file, what line).\n\nWhy two channels? Because:\n- A script that automates the program needs to branch on the *kind* of failure (usage vs data vs IO) — it can't parse text reliably (localization, formatting changes).\n- A human needs *details* to fix the problem — the exit code alone (\"65\") tells nothing about what went wrong.\n\nThe anti-pattern `return errno;`:\n- errno is a system-level error number (e.g., ENOENT=2, EACCES=13). Returning it directly is wrong because:\n  1. errno codes don't carry the project's semantic categories (64/65/66/74). E.g., errno 2 \"file not found\" could occur in the \"usage\" case and in the \"data\" case — the wrapper can't distinguish the error class.\n  2. errno is often not even set or is stale in many contexts.\n  3. errno numbers collide with project codes and with each other confusingly.\n  4. It leaks system implementation details into the machine protocol.\n\nSo the design is: the wrapper branches on the exit code (category), the human reads stderr (details). Each channel does what it's good at. Don't put human detail in the machine channel (can't fit), and don't make the machine channel depend on text.\n\nLet me use their own examples:\n- t3/t4: \"не удалось прочитать stdin: <системный текст> (errno <код>)\" in stderr, exit 74 for IO, 65 for non-number in stream. A test script (run_case / bat) checks exit code 65 without parsing the Russian text. A human reads the stderr for \"строка 1: ...\" details.\n- In run_case: it compares expected vs actual exit code — that's the machine channel doing its job. The mismatch message goes to stderr (human channel).\n- In make: `$(RUN_CASE) \"...\" 65 && touch` — make's test harness consumes the exit code as the machine channel; the stderr detail goes to the test log for the human.\n\nNow I should relate this to the earlier discussion about why test tools return 1: because make is the wrapper/consumer for the test tool, and make only needs \"passed/failed\" (a boolean), not a category. The task programs return categories (64/65/66/74) because their wrapper (run_case/bat) DOES need the category to check expected behavior.\n\nLet me write a clear explanation in Russian. The user wants me to \"поясни его ещё раз\" — explain it again. Keep it focused, use concrete examples from their code. I should be reasonably concise but this is a teaching request, so some depth is warranted. I'll use the anti-sycophancy format markers as required by the AGENTS.md §4.2 block (category/confidence markers).\n\nLet me structure:\n1. Definition of the two channels and their consumers.\n2. Why each carries what it carries.\n3. Anti-pattern `return errno;` — why it's wrong.\n4. Tie back to their concrete case (t3/t4 and test tools).\n\nKeep it as an explanation, no tools needed. This is a question, not a planning task requiring tools. I'm in plan mode — read-only, but this is just a text answer, no file changes needed.",
