ency on each other, and the missing define doesn't matter.\n\nBut that's a dangerous accident: for t3/t4 (which DO call parse_point), the missing /DCOMMON_STATIC on main would produce `__imp_` references. Let me think about what happens then:\n\n- main.obj: `parse_point` declared `__declspec(dllimport)`. Call site → `call [__imp_?parse_point...]`. Undefined external `__imp_?parse_point@@...`.\n- geometry_cpp.obj: defines `?parse_point@@...` plain (COMMON_STATIC → COMMON_API empty).\n\nWhen linking, the linker needs `__imp_?parse_point` — which isn't defined anywhere → LNK2019 unresolved external symbol __imp_... unless import lib. So it WOULD fail. Hence for t3/t4 the makefile currently (if extended naively) would break. That's exactly the static/DLL split concern we already flagged in the plan (need separate `_dll` objects with COMMON_EXPORTS, and main compiled with dllimport matching the import lib).\n\nBut wait — for t2, which calls string_utils functions (trim_str, is_empty): in make, t2 not yet implemented. If pattern rule applies without /DCOMMON_STATIC, t2 main would reference __imp_?trim_str → string_utils_cpp.obj defines plain trim_str → LNK2019. So t2 would break with current naive pattern rule. The fix: compile t2 main with /DCOMMON_STATIC, matching static build of string_utils.\n\nSo the user's observation is a real latent bug for t2/t3/t4, and t1 only survives by accident (no function calls into common).\n\nLet me verify: does the current Makefile even build t2/t3/t4? No — Makefile only has t1. So currently \"works\" = t1 only. And t1 works because no common function calls.\n\nLet me double-check the C side too. t1_dist_matrix_c/main.c: includes geometry.h, uses struct Point, cos/sin/sqrt/pow. Does it call parse_point? No. So same.\n\nNow — let me also reconsider: maybe the user is asking about something slightly different: \"у меня же компиляция самих задач была с флагом dcommon static, а сейчас этот флаг потерялся - все c / cpp массово перегоняются в объектики\". She's noting that in .bat, compilation used /DCOMMON_STATIC, but in Makefile the generic pattern rule doesn't have it. She asks why it works anyway.\n\nLet me verify my claim about __imp_ references by checking main.obj for any parse_point reference. The dumpbin showed no parse_point in main.obj (the grep on main.obj for \"parse_point|Point\" matched only Point-related std symbols, no parse_point). And geometry_cpp.obj defines parse_point. Confirmed: main.obj has no reference to parse_point.\n\nSo the answer:\n1. t1 \"works\" because it never calls a COMMON_API function — it only uses the `Point` struct, which is header-only and has no linkage requirements. COMMON_API macro affects function/export declarations; it doesn't touch the struct. So whether main is compiled with /DCOMMON_STATIC or not, there's no symbol to resolve — nothing to break.\n2. This is accidental and doesn't generalize: t2 (string_utils calls), t3/t4 (parse_point calls) DO call common functions. Without /DCOMMON_STATIC on main, calls would compile as `__declspec(dllimport)` → references to `__imp_?func@...`, which the static common .obj doesn't provide → LNK2019. So the naive pattern rule would break those.\n3. The correct design (already in the plan): static tasks (t1/t2) — main compiled with /DCOMMON_STATIC matching static common objects; DLL tasks (t3/t4) — common compiled with COMMON_EXPORTS into DLL + import lib, main compiled WITHOUT COMMON_STATIC (so COMMON_API = dllimport) and linked against import lib. The pattern rule must be refined to add per-task defines.\n\nWait — but the Makefile pattern rule: `$(BINDIR)\\\\%.obj: %.cpp | $(CPP_DIRS)` uses `$(CXX) /c /Fo:$@ $(CXXFLAGS) $<`. CXXFLAGS doesn't include /DCOMMON_STATIC. So if t2 main.cpp is compiled via this rule, it gets dllimport. That would break t2 when we add it. Unless we add /DCOMMON_STATIC to the pattern rule (but then t3/t4 main must NOT have it). So the pattern rule needs a define parameter per task, or separate handling.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider whether dllimport on a function called in main, linked against a static .obj that defines the plain symbol, actually fails. In MSVC, `__declspec(dllimport)` on a function: the compiler generates a call through `__imp_` pointer. At link time, the linker needs `__imp_?func@@...` symbol. If the object defines plain `?func@@...` but not `__imp_?func@@...`, you get LNK2019 \"unresolved external symbol __imp_?func@@\". Yes, that's a classic MSVC error. So t2/t3/t4 would fail.\n\nBUT actually there's a subtlety: does MSVC sometimes fall back? For functions, if you declare dllimport but the function is defined in the same TU or... no. The classic rule: dllimport function calls → __imp_ indirect. Linking against the real definition without an import library → LNK2019 on __imp_. There's an exception: linker warning LNK4217 \"locally defined symbol imported\" happens when... that's when you have both. Actually, the linker CAN resolve __imp_?func if it sees ?func defined and generates a thunk? No — that's what import libs are for. Actually, I recall LNK4217 warning: \"locally defined symbol ?func imported in function main\" — this occurs when the linker finds a local definition for a symbol that's also referenced via dllimport. Hmm. Let me think.\n\nActually, in MSVC, when you use dllimport on a function and then link against an object that defines it locally, the linker... I believe it resolves the __imp_ reference if the plain symbol is available? No. Let me think about the actual mechanism.\n\nThe dllimport function call is: `call qword ptr [__imp_?func]`. The linker needs `__imp_?func`. When linking a static lib that has `?func`, the linker... does NOT automatically create `__imp_?func`. Unless the compiler emits it. Actually there IS a known behavior: LNK4217 \"locally defined symbol imported\" and LNK4286... These occur when a locally-defined symbol is declared dllimport. The linker emits the warning but may still resolve it via the __imp_ thunk IF... hmm.\n\nActually, I recall: when you have dllimport + local definition, the MSVC linker will resolve the `__imp_` reference to the local definition by generating the import thunk? No. Let me recall LNK4217: \"locally defined symbol 'symbol' imported in function 'function'\". This warning is emitted when the linker finds a local definition of a symbol that is also declared dllimport, and it resolves the reference to the local definition (ignoring dllimport). Wait, that means it WOULD work but with a warning?\n\nHmm, actually for x64, dllimport function references become `__imp_?func` data references. If the linker finds `?func` defined locally, does it resolve `__imp_?func`? I believe the linker, upon seeing `__imp_?func` and a local `?func`, will generate an error or warning. Let me think about the documented behavior.\n\nMicrosoft docs: \"When you use __declspec(dllimport) with a function, and the function is defined locally (in the same program), the linker uses the local definition and emits warning LNK4217.\" Actually I recall the warning LNK4217 text: \"locally defined symbol 'symbol' imported in function 'function'\". And there's a fix guidance. \n\nHmm, I'm not 100% sure. For t1 it doesn't matter because there's no function call at all. So I don't need to resolve this definitively for t1. But for the plan I should note that t2/t3/t4 need correct defines.\n\nActually let me not overcomplicate. Let me focus on answering the user's specific question: \"почему это вообще работает\" for t1. The answer: because t1 main.cpp doesn't call any COMMON_API function — only uses the Point struct from the header. The struct needs no linkage; COMMON_API only decorates function declarations/exports. So the missing /DCOMMON_STATIC changes nothing observable in t1's objects: main.obj has no dependency on common functions, and geometry_cpp.obj compiled with COMMON_STATIC is just a normal static function definition. Link succeeds.\n\nLet me double-che