atrix_cpp\\main.obj: COMMON_FLAG := /DCOMMON_STATIC\n$(BINDIR)\\t2_passport_cpp\\main.obj: COMMON_FLAG := /DCOMMON_STATIC\n```\nThe main.obj targets are created by the pattern rule, but target-specific assignments still attach. This works. And common objects need explicit rules anyway (different source name → obj name mapping, e.g., geometry.cpp → geometry_cpp.obj), and they need TWO variants each.\n\nOption B: Fully explicit rules per obj. More verbose.\n\nActually, the target-specific variable approach is exactly the answer to her question. But I should be careful: is the answer \"yes you can\" but \"it doesn't remove the need for two common object variants\"? Let me articulate.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the design. Could we instead have a SINGLE common object compiled with no define? No — because COMMON_API defaults to dllimport when neither is defined. For static linking of a static lib, the common functions would be marked dllimport in their own implementation file... Actually let's think: `geometry.cpp` includes `geometry.hpp`. If compiled with no define, COMMON_API = `__declspec(dllimport)`. Then geometry.cpp itself would declare its own functions as dllimport AND define them — MSVC would complain or behave oddly. Actually defining a function that was declared dllimport in the same TU: the definition overrides, it becomes a normal function (actually there can be warnings/errors). Not clean.\n\nSo for the static case we MUST define COMMON_STATIC (empty API) in common objects. For the dll case we MUST define COMMON_EXPORTS. So two variants are unavoidable for common objects. This is a hard constraint.\n\nFor mains: t1/t2 mains must have COMMON_STATIC (so that calls to common functions are direct calls, not dllimport indirection — otherwise even though it links via the fallback... actually earlier analysis said t1 works because no calls; t2 calls string_utils so would produce `__imp_` references → link fail against static obj). t3/t4 mains should have