g those from pattern rules — the value applies when that target is being built. I recall you can do:\n\n```\n$(BINDIR)\\common\\geometry_cpp.obj: CXXFLAGS += /DCOMMON_STATIC\n```\n\nThis is a common idiom. Yes.\n\nOne more consideration: The order-only prerequisites and directory creation. Fine.\n\nLet me also reflect: The user asked \"а нельзя завести переменную с флагом dcommon static и менять её в зависимости от ситуации, давать ей разные флаги?\" — basically \"can't we have a variable holding the flag and give it different values per situation?\"\n\nAnswer: Yes — that's target-specific variable values in GNU make, a built-in feature designed exactly for this. Set a `COMMON_FLAG` variable, include it in the pattern rule's recipe, and override it per concrete target (or per task). This is cleaner than duplicating rules.\n\nBut: it does NOT solve the common-object two-variant problem. geometry_cpp.obj can only be built one way; t3/t4 need it with dllexport while t1 needs it static. So you still need `geometry_cpp.obj` (static) and a separate dll-variant object (e.g., `geometry_cpp_dll.obj`). The variable helps express flags, not multiplicity.\n\nI should also mention a subtlety: target-specific variables propagate to prerequisites. If you set `COMMON_FLAG` on the t1 aggregate phony, it would leak into all prereqs. So set it at the object level, or use the pattern. Actually careful: if we set it on `$(BINDIR)\\t1_dist_matrix_cpp\\main.obj`, that's the leaf target — no prereqs that compile code. Good.\n\nBut there's another subtlety: two mains (t1 cpp main and t1 c main) — different objects, each needs its own target-specific assignment. That's fine, just 2 lines per task.\n\nAlternatively, could use a `define`/template to generate the target-specific assignments — but that's over-engineering for 4-8 mains.\n\nLet me also think about whether the pattern rule even applies to main.obj for t1 currently. Yes — earlier we saw t1_cpp main.obj built via pattern rule with no