          "text": "The user asks: can I get the name of the target (e.g., t1) inside rules after invoking it, to do something like `ifeq(t1)... else ifeq(t2)...`?\n\nIn GNU make, there's no built-in \"current goal name\" variable that's guaranteed, but there are some mechanisms:\n\n1. `$(MAKECMDGOALS)` — the list of goals specified on the command line. So if you run `make t1`, `$(MAKECMDGOALS)` = `t1`. This can be used in `ifeq` conditionals.\n\n2. But there's a fundamental issue: `ifeq` is evaluated **when the makefile is read** (parse time), not at recipe execution time. So `ifeq ($(MAKECMDGOALS), t1)` works only for the static structure — it can conditionally define variables or rules at parse time based on goals.\n\n3. However, using `$(MAKECMDGOALS)` for conditional compilation flags is fragile and considered an anti-pattern by many: \n   - make can build multiple goals at once (`make all test`), and `MAKECMDGOALS` is a space-separated list — matching needs care.\n   - If a target is built as a prerequisite of another (not directly on command line), it won't be in `MAKECMDGOALS` at all. So `make test` → `test` depends on `.ok` files which depend on exes... the exe targets are NOT in MAKECMDGOALS. So `ifeq` based on MAKECMDGOALS can't distinguish which task an obj belongs to when built transitively.\n   - Recursive make would be needed to make per-goal configs work robustly.\n\nActually the key insight: the user wants to vary flags based on which task is being built. But the objects for t1 and t3 are both built in one `make test` run potentially (well, currently `all` builds t1; but in the future `all` builds t1..t4). If you build all four tasks in one make invocation, a single global variable cannot simultaneously be `/DCOMMON_STATIC` for t1 main and empty for t3 main. `MAKECMDGOALS`-based ifeq would set ONE value for the whole run — wrong when multiple tasks are built together.\n\nSo the answer: \n- Yes, there's `$(MAKECMDGOALS)` — the list of goals given on the command line.\n- You can write `ifeq ($(filter t1,$(MAKECMDGOALS)),t1)` style conditionals.\n- BUT it's evaluated at makefile-parse time and is global for the whole invocation; it cannot express per-target differences when multiple targets are built in one run (e.g. `make all test` or `all` covering t1..t4). It also doesn't see targets built as prerequisites.\n- So for the per-task flag problem, the correct mechanism is target-specific variables (which we discussed), not MAKECMDGOALS/ifeq.\n\nThis is the plan-mode context. I should answer the question, explain the mechanics, warn about the pitfalls, and relate it back to the plan (target-specific variables remain the right tool). Also I'm in plan mode — read-only, no edits. Just answer.\n\nLet me keep it concise but informative. Mark categories as per the anti-sycophancy block: [ФАКТ]/[МНЕНИЕ] and confidence.\n\nLet me make sure I'm accurate about MAKECMDGOALS:\n- `MAKECMDGOALS` — GNU make variable, space-separated list of goals given on the command line. Available since ancient make. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ]\n- Conditional `ifeq` is processed when the makefile is parsed. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ]\n- MAKECMDGOALS only contains targets explicitly named on the command line, NOT their prerequisites. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ]\n\nPitfalls:\n1. Multiple goals in one invocation.\n2. Prerequisites not in the list.\n3. Default goal (if no goal given, `all` — actually if no goals specified, MAKECMDGOALS is empty! Because MAKECMDGOALS contains only explicitly-specified goals. So `make` with default goal → MAKECMDGOALS empty.)\n4. Parse-time evaluation — you can't change flags \"during the build\" depending on which target is being updated.\n\nSo the conclusion: ifeq(MAKECMDGOALS, t1) can't solve the per-object flag issue. Target-specific variable values remain the correct mechanism.\n\nI should also mention: there's no built-in `$(TARGET)` / current-target variable accessible in recipes the way she imagines. There's `$@` (the target name) available in recipes — but it's the target being built, not the goal. In recipes you have `$@`, `$<`, `$^`. But `ifeq` doesn't see `$@` because ifeq is parse-time, while `$@` is recipe-time. That's the crux: ifeq happens too early to see per-target context. To vary behavior per target at recipe time you'd use target-specific variables or explicit rules.\n\nLet me write the answer.",
