          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user asks a conceptual question: \"I thought that what follows `:` after a target is always prerequisites. So target-specific variable is another category that can be after `:`?\"\n\nAnswer: Yes, exactly. After the colon in a rule line, GNU make distinguishes two things:\n1. Prerequisite list\n2. Target-specific variable assignment (var := value)\n\nHow does make distinguish? The manual says: if the text after the colon (after whitespace) looks like a variable assignment, it's a target-specific variable; otherwise it's a prerequisite list.\n\nActually, the syntax rule: `target : var = value` vs `target : prereq1 prereq2`. GNU make decides based on whether the second field is a variable assignment.\n\nLet me be precise. From the GNU make manual §6.11 (Target-specific Variable Values):\n> The syntax for a target-specific variable assignment is:\n> ```\n> target ... : variable-assignment\n> ```\n> ... The variable-assignment can be any valid form of assignment (recursive `=`, simple `:=`, append `+=`, conditional `?=`, or immediate `::=`).\n\nAnd §3.1 (What a Rule Looks Like):\n> A rule has this form:\n> ```\n> targets: prerequisites\n>         recipe\n> ```\n> or\n> ```\n> targets: prerequisites ; recipe\n>         recipe\n> ```\n\nSo yes — the text after `:` can be either prerequisites or a variable assignment. Make distinguishes them: \"If the text after the colon is a variable assignment, it's a target-specific variable, not a prerequisite list.\"\n\nThe key distinguishing point: GNU make determines this during parsing. The manual says (§6.11): \"A target-specific variable assignment looks like this: `target ... : variable-assignment` ... The variable-assignment is recognized by the fact that it's an assignment.\"\n\nActually, how does make tell `target: COMMON_FLAG := /DCOMMON_STATIC` from `target: COMMON_FLAG /DCOMMON_STATIC`? It parses the second field and checks if it's a valid variable assignment form (contains `=` as an assignment operator). So `var := value` is detected as assignment.\n\nSo the user's understanding is correct — after the colon there are two possible categories: prerequisites OR target-specific variable assignment. Make distinguishes them syntactically.\n\nThis is a read-only plan mode session; I just answer the question. Keep concise.",
