          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user asks: does COMMON_STATIC / COMMON_EXPORTS — specified at compile stage — control the type of linking (static/dynamic)?\n\nThis is a conceptual question. Let me answer precisely.\n\nThe macros COMMON_STATIC/COMMON_EXPORTS are used at compile time to control how `COMMON_API` is defined in the header:\n\n- `COMMON_STATIC` → `COMMON_API` is empty → the functions are ordinary functions, no `__declspec(dllimport/dllexport)`.\n- `COMMON_EXPORTS` → `COMMON_API = __declspec(dllexport)` → when compiling the DLL itself, mark functions for export.\n- neither → `COMMON_API = __declspec(dllimport)` → when compiling the consumer, mark functions as imported from a DLL.\n\nSo the macros control the decoration of the functions in the object code — whether the compiler emits `__imp_` references (dllimport) or not. The actual linking type (static lib vs DLL) is determined at link stage by what you pass to `link` (whether you link the .obj files directly / a static .lib, or link against an import .lib + have the DLL).\n\nSo: the macro is set at compile time, and it *prepares* the object code for a certain linkage mode, but it doesn't itself perform the linking. The link type is decided at link time. The macro and the link must agree — if you compile with dllimport (neither macro for the consumer) but link statically without import lib, you get unresolved `__imp_` symbols (or LNK4217/LNK2019). If you compile with empty API (COMMON_STATIC) but try to link against a DLL import lib, that also mismatches.\n\nSo the answer: partially correct. It's specified at compile time but it's about the *declaration of linkage* (import/export decoration), i.e. it tells the compiler how the functions will be linked, so that the compiler generates the right code (direct calls vs. calls through import thunks `__imp_`). The actual \"стат./динам.\" resolution happens at link time by what you feed to `link`. The macro is a *precondition/agreement* that must match the link stage.\n\nSo I should explain: it's specified at compile, controls the *kind of linkage* the functions will have (their calling convention through import pointer or direct), and the link step must match. So \"отвечает за вид линковки\" is roughly right in intent but the linkage is actually decided at link time; the macro only decorates symbols at compile time so that code generation is correct.\n\nAlso worth noting the difference between static linking and dynamic: \n- static: compile consumer with COMMON_STATIC (empty API), link .obj/.lib directly.\n- dynamic: compile DLL sources with COMMON_EXPORTS (dllexport) to build DLL + import lib; compile consumer without COMMON_STATIC (dllimport) and link with import lib; at runtime DLL present.\n\nThe phrase \"отвечает за вид линковки\" — yes, it's a marker that makes the *compiler* emit code consistent with one linkage style, but the linkage itself is done by `link`. So it's a \"compile-time declaration of linkage intent\".\n\nKeep concise. Mark [ФАКТ]/[МНЕНИЕ] per anti-sycophancy. Confidence.\n\nI'm in plan mode - read-only, just answering conceptual question.",
